/**
 * Standalone (no-collection) UVA problems, batch N (7 problems), under the upgraded 4-test-case
 * standard (Sample + boundary + tricky/adversarial case + stress test near the stated upper bound).
 *
 * IMPORTANT DATA-QUALITY NOTE: uva-11219-basic-wall-maze's stored title/slug do not match its actual
 * scraped content at all -- the statementMd and Sample are unambiguously about an AGE CALCULATOR
 * (given a current date and birth date, compute age in years, or report "Invalid birth date" / "Check
 * birth date"), not a maze of any kind. This looks like the same class of scraping mismatch found
 * earlier this session on uva-10405 (wrong title/slug attached to otherwise-correct content). Since I
 * have no reliable way to look up UVA's actual official title for this problem's real content without
 * external access, I did NOT rename the slug (to avoid compounding the error with a guessed name) --
 * this is flagged here for a human to verify against the real UVA source and rename if appropriate.
 * The test data below is fully verified against the actual (age-calculator) statement + sample content,
 * which is unambiguous and directly checkable regardless of what the row is titled.
 *
 * - uva-11121-base-2: standard base -2 conversion -- repeatedly take a non-negative remainder (0 or 1)
 *   mod 2, then divide by -2 (not 2), continuing until the value reaches 0. Verified against the
 *   official sample (4 cases including 0 and a negative input). Boundary: n=0 -> "0" specifically (the
 *   only value that terminates immediately with no digits, needing its own special case in the loop).
 *   Tricky: n=-1 -> "11" (hand-verified: 1 + 1*(-2) = -1), the smallest negative input exercising the
 *   remainder-adjustment step at all. Stress: both stated extremes, -1,000,000,000 and 1,000,000,000,
 *   in a single test case (N up to 10000 per the statement, though only 2 cases are needed to hit both
 *   bounds).
 * - uva-11157-dynamic-frog: modeled as a flow-feasibility problem -- binary search on the maximum leap
 *   distance L, and for each candidate L check whether 2 units of flow (there and back) can be pushed
 *   from the left bank to the right bank through a graph where edges connect any two rocks/banks within
 *   distance L, with small rocks split into an in/out node pair joined by a capacity-1 edge (used at
 *   most once across the ENTIRE round trip, not once per direction) while big rocks and the banks have
 *   unlimited capacity. Verified against the official sample (many cases, large inputs). Boundary: N=0
 *   (no rocks at all) forces a direct D-meter jump each way, answer = D trivially. Tricky: a single
 *   SMALL rock exactly at the midpoint of a river otherwise too wide to cross in one leap -- since it
 *   can only be used for ONE direction of the trip (not both), the other direction is forced into the
 *   full-width D jump, giving max(D/2, D) = D, not D/2 as a "reuse each rock per direction" bug would
 *   wrongly compute. Stress: N=100 (stated max) stones alternating small/big, D near the stated 10^9
 *   bound.
 * - uva-11175-from-d-to-e-and-back: applies Heuchenne's theorem (the classical necessary-and-sufficient
 *   characterization of line digraphs): E is a valid line digraph of some D iff, for every pair of
 *   E-edges sharing a common target vertex, their two source vertices have IDENTICAL out-neighbor sets.
 *   Verified against the full official sample (4 cases, including one genuine violation and one dense
 *   valid case). Boundary: the trivial single-vertex, zero-edge graph (vacuously satisfies the
 *   condition, Yes). Tricky: a 4-vertex graph where two edges share a target but their sources have
 *   out-neighbor sets of clearly different SIZES ({2,3} vs {3}), an unambiguous violation, hand-traced
 *   before trusting the code's "No" verdict. Stress: a graph constructed FORWARD from a genuine directed
 *   graph D (a simple 150-vertex cycle), guaranteeing validity by construction rather than by trusting
 *   the checking algorithm on an input whose ground truth I'd otherwise have to independently verify.
 * - uva-1118-binary-stirling-numbers: the parity of S(n,m) was NOT derivable directly from the given
 *   recurrence at the stated scale (n up to 10^9), so several candidate closed-form parity rules (based
 *   on Lucas' theorem for binomial coefficient parity) were tested against a brute-force DP table for
 *   small n before finding one with zero mismatches: S(n,m) is odd iff C(n-floor(m/2)-1, ceil(m/2)-1)
 *   is odd (itself checked via Lucas' theorem: odd iff the second argument's set bits are a subset of
 *   the first's). Confirmed 0 mismatches for every (n,m) with 1<=m<=n<=200 before trusting it on the
 *   official sample and large inputs. Boundary: n=m=1 (trivially 1 way to partition a single element
 *   into 1 part, odd). Tricky: n=m (at any scale) is always exactly 1 way (every element its own
 *   singleton part), hence always odd -- independent of the parity-formula derivation, a fact from the
 *   combinatorial definition itself. Stress: n=10^9, m=5*10^8 (large, near the stated bound).
 * - uva-11192-group-reverse: split the string into G equal-length contiguous groups and reverse each
 *   group in place. Verified against the official sample (many lines). Boundary: G=1 (the whole string
 *   is one group, fully reversed). Tricky: G equal to the string's own length (each group is exactly 1
 *   character), which must be a complete no-op (reversing a 1-character "group" changes nothing).
 *   Stress: a 100-character string (stated max length) with G=9 (largest G under the stated bound of
 *   10).
 * - uva-11219-basic-wall-maze (mislabeled; see note above -- actual content is an age calculator):
 *   parse both dates as (year,month,day) tuples; if the birth date is chronologically after the current
 *   date, "Invalid birth date"; otherwise age = currentYear-birthYear, decremented by 1 if the current
 *   (month,day) tuple is strictly earlier than the birth (month,day) tuple (i.e. this year's birthday
 *   hasn't been reached yet); "Check birth date" if the result exceeds 130, otherwise print the number
 *   (0 is a valid, printable age, not a special "Invalid" case). Verified against the full official
 *   sample (22 cases, including several Feb-29-birthday edge cases already exercising the leap-year
 *   boundary logic). Boundary: identical current and birth dates -> age 0. Tricky: current date
 *   01/03/2001 (a non-leap year, so no Feb 29 exists) against a birth date of 29/02/2000 (a leap-year
 *   Feb 29 birthday) -- since the current MONTH (3) already exceeds the birth month (2), the birthday
 *   has unambiguously passed this year regardless of the exact day, giving age 1 (not adjusted down),
 *   confirming the month-first tuple comparison handles this correctly without needing to reconcile
 *   Feb 29 against a non-leap current year at all. Stress: T=200 (stated max) varied valid date pairs.
 * - uva-11220-decoding-the-message: within each line, maintain a "required letter index" starting at 1;
 *   walk through the line's words in order, and whenever a word has at least that many letters, take
 *   the letter at that index and advance the required index by 1 (otherwise skip the word without
 *   advancing); the line's decoded word is the concatenation of every letter actually taken. Verified
 *   against the full official sample (8 test cases), including hand-tracing the exact multi-word-skip
 *   mechanics of several sample lines (e.g. "as I previously previewed" needing 2 consecutive words
 *   skipped for index 2 before "previously" finally supplies it) before trusting the extraction logic.
 *   Boundary: a single one-word line ("x") decodes to just "x". Tricky: "a bb c d e" -- index 1 taken
 *   from "a", index 2 taken from "bb", then indices 3/4/5 all fail against the remaining 1-letter words
 *   "c","d","e" in turn (each too short), exhausting the line with only 2 letters decoded despite 5
 *   words being present, confirming skips don't wrongly retry against an EARLIER already-passed word.
 *   Stress: N=100 lines (stated max) x M=30 words per line (stated max) x 30-letter words (stated max
 *   word length), all identical all-'a' words so every requested index up to 30 is always satisfiable.
 */
